C_p, the specific heat of gas at constant pressure, is defined as the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of gas through 1^0 K when pressure remains constant
C_{\nu}, the specific heat of gas at constant volume, is defined as the quantity of heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of gas through 1^0 K when volume remains constant.
When pressure is kept constant the volume of the gas increases with increase in temperature. Thus additional heat is required to increase the volume of gas against the external pressure. Therefore heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of gas through 1^0 K when pressure is kept constant is greater than the heat required when volume is kept constant. i.e. C_p &gt; C_v.
a) Effect of pressure on velocity of sound
According to Laplace's formula velocity of sound in air is
v = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P}{\rho}}
If M is the mass and V is volume of air then
\rho = \frac{M}{V}
\therefore \mathbf{v} = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma PV}{M}} \qquad --- (8.12)
At constant temperature PV = constant according to Boyle's law. Also M and \gamma are constant, hence v = constant.
Therefore at constant temperature, a change in pressure has no effect on velocity of sound in air. This can be seen in another way. For gaseous medium, PV = nRT, n being the number of moles.
\therefore \mathbf{v} = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma nRT}{M}} \qquad --- (8.13)
Hence for gaseous medium obeying ideal gas equation change in pressure has no effect on velocity of sound unless there is change in temperature.
Example 8.3: Consider a closed box of rigid walls so that the density of the air inside it is constant. On heating, the pressure of this enclosed air is increased from P_0 to P. It is now observed that sound travels 1.5 times faster than at pressure P_0 calculate P/P_0.
Solution:
\begin{aligned} \mathbf{v}_{P} &amp;= \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P_{o}}{\rho}} \\ \mathbf{v}_{Po} &amp;= \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P_{o}}{\rho}} \\ \mathbf{v}_{P} &amp;= 1.5 \, \mathbf{v}_{Po} \\ \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P}{\rho}} &amp;= 1.5 \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P_{o}}{\rho}} \\ \frac{P}{\rho} &amp;= 2.25 \frac{P_{o}}{\rho} \\ P &amp;= 2.25 \, P_{o} \end{aligned}
(b) Effect of temperature on speed of sound
Suppose v_0 and v are the speeds of sound at T_0 and T in kelvin respectively. Let \rho_0 and \rho be the densities of gas at these two temperatures. The velocity of sound at temperature T_0 and T can be written by using Eq. (8.13),
v_0 = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T_0}{M}} - M \text{ is molar mass, } n = 1
v = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma R T}{M}}
\therefore \frac{v}{v_0} = \sqrt{\frac{R T}{R T_0}}
\therefore \frac{v}{v_0} = \sqrt{\frac{T}{T_0}} - (8.14)
This equation shows that speed of sound in air is directly proportional to the square root of absolute temperature. Thus, speed of sound in air increases with increase in temperature. Taking T_o = 273 \, \text{K} and writing T = (273 + t) \, \text{K} where t is the temperature in degree celsius. The ratio of velocity of sound in air at t ^0C to that at 0^0C is given by,
\therefore \frac{V}{V_0} = \sqrt{\frac{273 + t}{273}}

\therefore \frac{V}{V_0} = \sqrt{1 + \frac{t}{273}}

\therefore \frac{V}{V_0} = \sqrt{1 + \alpha t} \quad \text{where } \alpha = \frac{1}{273}

or, V = V_0 \left(1 + \alpha t\right)^{\frac{1}{2}}
As \alpha is very small, we can write
V \approx V_0 \left(1 + \frac{1}{2}\alpha t\right)

V = V_0 \left(1 + \frac{1}{2} \times \frac{1}{273}t\right)

V = V_0 \left(1 + \frac{t}{546}\right)

V = V_0 + \frac{V_0}{546}t

But V_0 = 332 \text{ m/s at } 0^{\circ}\text{C}
\therefore V = V_0 + \frac{332}{546}t

\therefore V \approx V_0 + (0.61)t, \qquad --- (8.15)
i.e., for 1^{\circ}C rise in temperature velocity increases by 0.61 m/s. Hence for small variations in temperature (&lt; 50^{\circ} C), the speed of sound changes linearly with temperature.
(c) Effect of humidity on speed of sound
Humidity (moisture) in air depends upon the presence of water vapour in it. Let \rho_m and \rho_d be the densities of moist and dry air respectively. If v_m and v_d are the speeds of sound in moist air and dry air then using Eq. (8.11).
v_{m} = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P}{\rho_{m}}}
and v_{d} = \sqrt{\frac{\gamma P}{\rho_{d}}}
\therefore \frac{v_{m}}{v_{d}} = \sqrt{\frac{\rho_{d}}{\rho_{m}}} \qquad --- (8.16)
Moist air is always less dense than dry air, i.e.,
\rho_{m} &lt; \rho_{d}
(\rho_{m} = 0.81 \text{ kg/m}^{3}(\text{at } 0^{\circ}\text{C}) \text{ and}
\rho_{d} = 1.29 \text{ kg/m}^{3}(\text{at } 0^{\circ}\text{C}\therefore v_{m} &gt; v_{d}.
Thus, the speed of sound in moist air is greater than speed of sound in dry air. i.e speed increases with increase in the moistness of air.

Consider a listener approaching with velocity v_L towards a stationary source S as shown in Fig. 8.2 (b). Let the first wave be emitted by the source at t=0, when the listener was at L_1 at an initial distance d from the source. Let t_1 be the instant when the listener receives this (wave), his position being L_2. During time t_1, the listener travels distance v_L t_1 towards the stationary source. In this time, the sound wave travels distance (d-v_1t_1) with speed v_L
\therefore t_1 = \frac{d - \mathbf{v}_L t_1}{\mathbf{v}} \quad \therefore t_1 = \frac{d}{\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{v}_L}
Second wave is emitted by the source at t = T_0 = the time period of the waves emitted by the source. Let t_2 be the instant when the listener receives second wave. During time t_2, the distance travelled by the listener is v_L t_2. Thus, the distance to be travelled by the sound to reach the listener is then d - v_I t_2.
 Sound (second wave) travels this distance with speed v in time = \frac{d - v_L t_2}{v_L}
However, this time should be counted after T_0, as the second wave was emitted at t=T_0.
\therefore t_2 = T_0 + \frac{d - \mathbf{v}_L t_2}{\mathbf{v}} \qquad \therefore t_2 = \frac{\mathbf{v} T_0 + d}{\mathbf{v} + \mathbf{v}_L}
Similarly, t_3 = 2T_0 + \frac{d - v_L t_3}{v} \therefore t_3 = \frac{2vT_0 + d}{v + v_L} Extending this argument to (p+1)^{th} wave, we can write,
t_{p+1} = pT_0 + \frac{d - v_L t_{p+1}}{v} \therefore t_{p+1} = \frac{p v T_0 + d}{v + v_L}
Time duration between instances of receiving successive waves is the observed or recorded period T.
\therefore pT = t_{p+1} - t_1 = \frac{p v T_0 + d}{v + v_L} - \frac{d}{v + v_L} = \frac{p v T_0}{v + v_L}
\therefore T = T_0 \left( \frac{v}{v + v_L} \right) \qquad - (8.22)
\therefore \frac{1}{T} = \frac{1}{T_0} \left( \frac{v + v_L}{v} \right)
\therefore n = n_0 \left( \frac{v + v_L}{v} \right) \qquad - (8.23)
